const CHANGES=[
  ['2026.09.17.0',[
    'New: a line at the top of the Latin front screen, until 6 October &mdash; Greek first, forty-five minutes there before this deck, whatever this count says. Latin has around 2,000 words ready and Greek a fraction of that, so the bigger number sits on the deck the diagnostic does not test, and the bigger number is what decides which icon gets tapped. It appears on Latin only, there is nothing to dismiss and nothing is stored, and it stops appearing by itself once 6 October is past.'
  ]],
  ['2026.09.16.1',[
    'Removed: importing a word list. Settings no longer offers Import a word list or an extraction key, and the screen that read a spreadsheet, a PDF, a Word file or a pasted list has gone with it. It was built for somebody bringing their own vocabulary to the app rather than for the two decks here, and nothing you have studied is affected.',
    'Changed: this list now shows the last ten builds rather than every build since July. Both deck files were carrying the whole history &mdash; about a fifth of a megabyte the app read on every launch.'
  ]],
  ['2026.09.16.0',[
    'Changed: the buttons on the study screen no longer rise when the mouse points at them. On a laptop, Again, Hard, Good, Easy, Show answer and Knew it all lifted slightly under the pointer. Each button also answers to a patch of screen larger than itself, reaching above and below it, and the Show answer bar sits inside the grade buttons&rsquo; patches &mdash; so pressing Show answer left the pointer resting on one grade, and that grade stayed raised on every card until the mouse was moved, which read as the app suggesting an answer. It never was: nothing here predicts your grade, and the only button that ever marks itself out is Easy, ringed while you hold Space. Pressing a button still darkens it.'
  ]],
  ['2026.09.14.0',[
    'Fixed: the small dark dot at the bottom of every screen. It was the bar that appears after you tie two words, drawn empty ever since the 8 September update &mdash; meant to stay hidden between uses, it sat on top of everything instead. On a phone it covered part of Show answer and the inner edges of Hard and Good, and a tap that landed on it did nothing. The bar still appears after a tie, as before.'
  ]],
  ['2026.09.09.0',[
    'New: a Tied words screen, on the front screen under the word list. It gives you one word at a time and offers the meanings of everything it is tied to as the answers &mdash; so the wrong answer is always the word you actually confuse it with &mdash; then shows the whole group side by side. That second half is the part that does the work. Your ties did nothing at all before this: the only thing one had ever changed was which words came back from the suspended pile on the same day, and none of yours had both words suspended.',
    'It is not review. Nothing on that screen changes when a word is next due, and nothing goes into your record. Picking a meaning out of two is easier than recalling it cold, and counting those as the same event would quietly corrupt the scheduler&rsquo;s picture of what you know. The screen keeps its own tally instead, on the tie rather than on either word, and uses it to put the ones you keep missing first next time.',
    'New: after a miss it asks what tells the two apart, with the answer still in front of you. That question used to be asked while you were tying words &mdash; in the middle of sorting through dozens of them, at which point you rarely know yet. 165 of your 177 ties have no line on them; this is where they get written.',
    'Changed: where two words genuinely have the same meaning &mdash; the deck gives both nefarius and scelestus as &ldquo;wicked&rdquo; &mdash; either answer counts, and the screen says why rather than marking one wrong.',
    'Changed: the pairs list is gone from the word list&rsquo;s Tied button. Reading down a list of ties was the weaker half of what it did, and the new screen does the stronger half properly; deleting a whole tie and merging tie chains both moved there with it. Tied only and Not tied stay as filters, and still combine with Part, Progress, Flag and the search box.',
    'Fixed: the Merge and Not now buttons on the tie-chain offer were unstyled text. They were added yesterday to a screen whose button style is scoped to dialogs.'
  ]],
  ['2026.09.08.0',[
    'Fixed: on a phone, "Tie to another word…" and Rename appeared to do nothing. Both open a dialog from inside the word panel, and the panel was painting over the top of it &mdash; measured at phone width, none of either dialog was reachable, which is why the panel had to be closed by hand before the dialog underneath could be seen. The panel now steps aside while the dialog is up and comes back the moment it closes, showing whatever you just changed. On a laptop nothing moves: there the panel is a column beside the list and the dialog always showed beside it.',
    'Changed: a word you have just tied keeps its place in the list. It used to disappear the instant the tie was made &mdash; and so did its partner &mdash; because it no longer matched "Not tied", which meant the one thing you usually want next, changing its flag, needed the filter cleared to get the word back. Tied words now stay where they are, struck through, until you change a filter or press the Clear beside the word count. Nothing below them moves, so your place in a long list is kept.',
    'New: after a tie, a bar names what was tied and carries a flag picker and an Undo. The flag saves the trip back to the word; the Undo is for a mistyped search, and appears only when the tie was newly made.',
    'New: where several of your ties are really one group joined a pair at a time &mdash; circa, circiter and circum tied as two separate pairs rather than one group of three &mdash; the Tied list offers to merge them, and says how many it found. Any line you had written about a pair is carried over. Nothing is merged without asking, and the offer disappears once taken.'
  ]],
  ['2026.09.02.1',[
    'New: hold Space, and the word is recorded Easy. Keep the key down through the reveal and let go when you have read the answer; a tap still reveals, and a second tap still records Good. On a phone the same gesture is a press and hold anywhere on the card once the answer is showing. Nothing you already do changes &mdash; letting go early records nothing at all.',
    'New: the Easy button lights up while you hold, in the colour of the grade that is actually going to be recorded. The five-second rule still applies exactly as it does to the button, so on a word that took you a while to turn over the button turns green rather than purple and the word is recorded Good &mdash; and now you can see that before you let go, instead of reading about it afterwards.',
    'Fixed: holding Space used to walk through the queue. Nothing filtered the key repeat, so a key left down alternated reveal and Good about twice a second, recording words you never saw. It is refused outright now.',
    'Changed: on a phone the line naming the last word rated is two rows by design. The word goes on top, muted, with the grade and its interval beside it; the answer sits underneath with the width to itself. Every word in both decks was measured at phone width: the old single line ran onto a second row for more than a third of them, and for almost all of them when it had the five-second note to carry, which is what made the card jump between words. It no longer moves. The laptop view is unchanged.',
    'Changed: the grade on that line is now said in colour as well as in words &mdash; a rule down its left edge, in the same colour that grade has always had. The words stay because the line is drawn while you are looking at the next question, where the four coloured buttons are not on screen to say what the colour means.',
    'Changed: Settings has a switch for the hold, under &ldquo;Hold to record Easy&rdquo;. Turn it off if you use VoiceOver, whose own press-and-hold means something else.'
  ]],
  ['2026.09.02.0',[
    'Changed: the app now keeps one line, in the card, naming the word you last rated &mdash; its answer, the grade recorded, and how far it jumped. It sits directly above the word you are reading, it appears whether you revealed the word or pressed &ldquo;Knew it&rdquo; without looking, and it stays until you rate the next one. Nothing to catch in time.',
    'Changed: it replaces two things that said overlapping halves of it from opposite corners of the screen. The answer to a &ldquo;Knew it&rdquo; press used to flash in the top-left corner for under half a second; the word it belonged to, with its rating and new interval, sat in the top-right on the &ldquo;Last card&rdquo; panel. Measured on a 1440&times;900 laptop, that was 726 pixels from the word you were reading to one of them and 1,240 pixels between the two, inside a 480ms window. The line is 133 pixels away and does not expire. The &ldquo;Last card&rdquo; panel is gone; everything it said is on the line.',
    'Changed: the line is set in the card&rsquo;s own type rather than the interface&rsquo;s. The old flash rendered an English gloss in the interface font while the card itself is in Palatino, which is why it never quite looked like part of the card.',
    'Changed: the toast reading &ldquo;Recorded as Good &mdash; that one took a while&rdquo; is gone. A press over the five-second line is still recorded as Good, and the line now says so in as many words, on both paths, for as long as the card is up &mdash; where the toast said it for 1.6 seconds and only if you had revealed the word first.',
    'Changed: two of the four study card shapes are retired. <em>Swap</em> revealed by shrinking the word up into the reserved row above it &mdash; the row the new line now occupies permanently, and the two cannot both have it. <em>Two panes</em>, the side-by-side wide layout, was the shape the centred ones had already replaced in use. What is left is <em>Replace</em>, which fades the answer in where the word was, and <em>Column</em>, which keeps the word above the answer. A deck set to either retired shape opens in Replace; before a reveal, Replace and Swap were identical anyway.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.30.10',[
    'Changed: the study screen no longer shows a word&rsquo;s review history while you are still trying to remember it. The line of chips beside the prompt &mdash; reviewed 24&times;, streak 0, last Again 15 days ago &mdash; is gone. It was a recognition cue standing in front of a recall test, and it was possible to read the answer&rsquo;s difficulty off it before attempting the word. The same figures are still in Browse, better ordered, where nothing is being tested. The card you just rated still appears in the corner: that one describes the previous word and cannot tell you anything about this one.',
    'Changed: with it go the two settings that rode alongside it. Keyboard hints are now simply on for every wide screen, and the two small prompt animations have been removed. There is no evidence they helped &mdash; Anki ships no interface motion at all &mdash; and one switch that quietly governed three unrelated things was never a good way to ask. The &ldquo;Study screen, wide layout&rdquo; setting is gone.',
    'Changed: a word&rsquo;s numbers in Browse now lead with the figure that actually tells words apart. The panel used to open &ldquo;Reviewed 24&times;&rdquo; and then, in the same sentence, say &ldquo;24 reviews since 72 days ago&rdquo; &mdash; the same number twice. It now reads the number of times you have pressed Again, then the review count once, then the streak; the timeline drops to a quieter second line. Reviews and streak barely separate one word from another; the Again count separates them a great deal.',
    'New: the Flag menu in Browse says what your flags are called. It listed seven coloured circles and their digits, with the names you had written stored where only a screen reader could reach them, while Part and Progress beside it both name and count every choice. It now does the same &mdash; swatch, name, and how many words carry it &mdash; and carries a Rename&hellip; row, so naming a flag no longer means opening a word first.',
    'New: a word&rsquo;s panel can untie it. Until now the only way out of a tie was a right-click on the row (a long press on a phone) followed by two more steps through a dialog. Untie now sits on the tie itself, next to Edit. It removes just that word from just that tie, and warns you first only when doing so would dissolve the pair and lose the line you wrote about it.',
    'New: the pairs list can delete a tie outright, with a Delete beside Add. Untie is about one word; this is about the pair. Both words stay in the deck exactly as they are &mdash; a tie has never affected when a word comes back.',
    'Changed: Tied is a proper filter now, with four choices instead of on and off. Any, Tied only, Not tied, and the pairs list. The first three combine with Part, Progress, Flag and the search box like every other filter, so you can ask for a flag&rsquo;s words that have no partner yet and watch the list shrink as you tie them. The pairs list still ignores every filter, deliberately &mdash; a tie is a relation, not a property of one word.',
    'Changed: the word list has its own button on the Home screen, the width of Start and directly under it. It used to be a small pill halfway down the page, from when it was a list of words and nothing else; it now carries the filters, the flags, the ties and the word panel.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.30.9',[
    'Fixed: pressing the number itself in the bring-back stepper quietly lowered it, the same as pressing &minus;. The hold-to-accelerate handler for &minus;/+ was scoped to anything carrying a marker attribute inside the stepper, and the number carries one too, for the separate click-to-type feature two builds ago &mdash; so pressing the number fired the decrement before that feature ever ran. Scoped to the two buttons themselves now: the number does only what it says, and opens for typing untouched.',
    'Changed: the bring-back number, in Settings and on the Home line, now sizes <em>one release</em> rather than capping the whole day. &ldquo;Bring back N words&rdquo; used to go quiet until tomorrow the moment N had gone out; it now reappears right away offering another batch the same size, and nothing stops you pressing it again as many times as the pile has words left. The one ceiling that remains is the one this always shared with new words on purpose: a returning word still costs what a new word costs, so a day already full of new material still makes the pile wait, however many times you ask.'
  ]],
  ['Earlier',[
    'Builds before this are not listed in the app. The full history from 30 July 2026 onward is kept with the project source.'
  ]]
];
